  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The isomers of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>, that can be prepared by&nbsp;Gabriel phthalimide synthesis are primary amines.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0065_a4e3f03c9bbb.png\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 115px;\" />&nbsp;</p><p>can be prepared by Gabriel phthalimide synthesis because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></math> is possible in</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0065_29102e83a76d.png\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 116px;\" /></p><p>Secondary&nbsp;amines react&nbsp;with Hinsberg&rsquo;s reagent to give solid insoluble in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaOH</mi></math>. Hence, Q is the secondary amine</p><p><img height=\"160\" src=\"assets\\st0065_bc5bb6cc39df.png\" width=\"213\" /></p><p>Aromatic amines like aniline give diazonium salts. The&nbsp;diazonium&nbsp;salt used in diazo coupling to get red dye with beta Naphthol in sodium hydroxide. Hence, R is aromatic amine as shown below.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0065_b4d0ffe4c045.png\" style=\"width: 275px; height: 161px;\" /></p></blockquote>"
